  "content": {
    "id": "p3-q4-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "Consider points $A(0,3)$ and $B(1,6)$, and the parabola with equation $y=x^2$. Find points $P$ on the parabola such that $AP=BP$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(2,4)$ and $\\left(-\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{49}{9}\\right)$.\n\nEquate the squared distances from $P(x,x^2)$ to $A$ and $B$.",
      "worked": "Write $P=(x,x^2)$. Since $AP=BP$, their squared distances are equal.\n$$\\begin{align*}\nx^2+(x^2-3)^2&=(x-1)^2+(x^2-6)^2\\\\\nx^4-5x^2+9&=x^4-11x^2-2x+37\\\\\n6x^2+2x-28&=0\\\\\n3x^2+x-14&=0\\\\\n(3x+7)(x-2)&=0\\\\\nx&=-\\frac{7}{3}\\text{ or }2.\n\\end{align*}$$\nFor $x=2$, $y=2^2=4$. For $x=-\\frac{7}{3}$, $y=\\left(-\\frac{7}{3}\\right)^2=\\frac{49}{9}$.\n\nTherefore, $P=(2,4)$ or $P=\\left(-\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{49}{9}\\right)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 72
  },
